BIT-17: Copy every pipe's segments into contiguous memory (more memory)

Segments come from a last-in first-out free list, so a pipe's chain ends
up scattered across memory, and every step walks every chain. Once per
hydraulic step (not at time 0), compactSegs() copies each pipe's and
tank's segments, in order, into a second memory pool and swaps the pools.
Contents are copied bit for bit; only addresses change.

It copies a segment with one memcpy, which is correct only because BIT-09
allocates a segment and its concentration arrays as one block: do not
apply it without BIT-09.

Optional: peak memory grows by 30-80 % on the larger cases, while the
patches before it change peak memory by at most a few megabytes. It is
last in the series so that it can be left out.

Requires: BIT-09
Results: identical (every concentration, the output file and the report are unchanged)

Apply from the root of an EPANET-MSX checkout at 85a303d, after FIX-01, FIX-02 and BIT-01 to BIT-16:
    git apply --whitespace=nowarn BIT-17.patch
or use tools/build-msx.sh, which applies the series in order.

